    question: "Design Search Autocomplete",
    slug: "sap-design-search-autocomplete-86",
    shortDescription: "Design low-latency autocomplete.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAutocomplete indexes terms or entities so prefix queries can return suggestions with low latency. A trie, search index, or specialized autocomplete service can be used depending on scale and ranking needs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks architecture judgment: requirements, bottlenecks, failure modes, consistency, and operational cost. The best answer explains why the components fit together instead of naming many services.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Clarify the core functional requirement.\n2. State the most important scale and reliability assumptions.\n3. Describe the request or data flow.\n4. Identify the bottleneck, failure mode, and key trade-off.\n5. Explain how you would validate the design.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would start with the requirement and the assumption that drives the architecture. Then I would walk through the main request or data flow, call out the likely bottleneck and failure mode, and finish with how I would measure the design in production.\n\n**Example :**\nFor prefix `jav`, return popular matches such as `javascript`, `java`, and `java spring` using a precomputed index and popularity score.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nThe read path benefits from precomputation and caching because the same prefixes are often requested repeatedly.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Drawing components before stating requirements.\n- Claiming that a system “scales” without identifying what limits it.\n- Ignoring consistency, retries, idempotency, observability, or operational ownership.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAutocomplete indexes terms or entities so prefix queries can return suggestions with low latency. A trie, search index, or specialized autocomplete service can be used depending on scale and ranking needs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks architecture judgment: requirements, bottlenecks, failure modes, consistency, and operational cost. The best answer explains why the components fit together instead of naming many services.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Clarify the core functional requirement.\n2. State the most important scale and reliability assumptions.\n3. Describe the request or data flow.\n4. Identify the bottleneck, failure mode, and key trade-off.\n5. Explain how you would validate the design.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would start with the requirement and the assumption that drives the architecture. Then I would walk through the main request or data flow, call out the likely bottleneck and failure mode, and finish with how I would measure the design in production.\n\n**Example :**\nFor prefix `jav`, return popular matches such as `javascript`, `java`, and `java spring` using a precomputed index and popularity score.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nThe read path benefits from precomputation and caching because the same prefixes are often requested repeatedly.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Drawing components before stating requirements.\n- Claiming that a system “scales” without identifying what limits it.\n- Ignoring consistency, retries, idempotency, observability, or operational ownership.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
