    shortDescription: "Tell me about a production incident.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nExplain the incident using impact, detection, mitigation, root cause, and prevention. Avoid turning the answer into a blame narrative.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is intended to reveal how you reason about an engineering situation, not just whether you know the vocabulary. A strong answer makes the assumption, decision, and outcome explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the situation or goal.\n2. Explain the decision or mechanism.\n3. Give the evidence or example that supports it.\n4. State the limitation, trade-off, or follow-up action.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer focused on the actual situation, then explain the decision and why I made it. I would finish with the result or lesson and let the interviewer decide whether to go deeper.\n\n**Example :**\nDescribe a spike in error rates, the rollback or mitigation you used to restore service, the root cause identified afterward, and the monitoring or test added to prevent recurrence.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIncident answers reveal how you behave under pressure and whether you learn from operational failures instead of only fixing the immediate symptom.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving a hypothetical story when a real example is expected.\n- Focusing on the team outcome without explaining your own contribution.\n- Leaving out the reasoning behind the decision.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
